The ratio is not defensible. A competent engineer would have skipped:

- **Cycles 128, 130 and 132 entirely.** Each built or repaired the build pipeline (binder, memory model, tunnel-name gate, rebase, FS-map carry) so that the next structural edit could pass its own checks (`STATUS.md:58-62,70-75,85-89`). None brought a run closer.
- **The three-way tunnel-naming investigation** across cycles 129 to 131 (`STATUS.md:64,74,79`). Its product is a rule about how LabVIEW names a tunnel, consumed only by the simulator.
- **The P3b split into halves sized by a memory model** (`docs/d1/INDEX.md:137-141`). The model exists because whole-VI reads during a build cost 2.53 MB each. A build that reads less would not need the model.
- **The card chat-D1 documentation reorganisation** run between cycles 129 and 130 (`STATUS.md:7-8`), a plan index and freeze of plan files that still describe an unrun chain.

Project scale is now 324 op VIs, 198 recipes and 1,052 peer exchanges behind one measured improvement.

## 4. Ordering at project scale

The work order is unchanged and still wrong. `STATUS.md:56-57` fixes it as P3b-2, then P4, P5, P6, then an interface-contract step, then the other tracks. PD281(b) already predicts P3b-2 may exceed the 690 MB stop, which would force a third half and another user decision (`docs/d1/ring-p3b.md:131-133`). The first run of the chain is still P6, now at least three steps and one likely re-split away.

What should come first, as the last review said and nothing changed:

- **R8, in-VI frame-rate control** on the display-loop VI, so 150 Hz can be measured at all.
- **The hand-off of the display-loop VI.** It is the one artefact that beat the original and it lives only in an archive file.
- **One 150 Hz run** to show whether tracking cost is the next limit before another $160 goes into the ring.

The steering mechanism meant to enforce this has fired nine times and been marked FOLLOWED nine times (`cycle_runner.log:242,293,333,455,528,629,694,768,932`). Cycle 128 was "FOLLOWED" while four of its five cards were offline tool cards (`STATUS.md:85-88`). The chat's own hand-off notes that the steering check cannot fail (`docs/chat-handoff.md:135`). FOLLOWED is a label, not a change of act.

## 5. Stale decisions

`decisions_pending.json` has one open item, D-2026-10-02-01, a guard hook for helper agents (`:286-297`). It is about process. The stale items are still the ones never asked:

