The user was reachable throughout: they relaunched the cycles on 10-01 and approved the doc reorganisation on 10-02 (`STATUS.md:7-9`). What they have not been shown is that four reviews in a row say the cycles are not moving the deliverable. The 10-01 review's "What was done with it" is blank (`archive/peer/2026-10-02-retrospective-cycle128.md:160`). The 09-28 review's annotation cites a 2026-09-23 ruling that repetition does not stop the runner (`archive/peer/2026-09-28-outcome-review-20260928.md:235`). That ruling predates all four refutations and has never been put back to the user with these numbers.

## 6. The experiment test

**The original.** The display-loop VI is the only alternative, and it has never run with a real sample, its longest run is 120 s, the user has not been told what the file is, it has no in-VI frame-rate control, and STATUS says it is not used in experiments until the user says so (`archive/2026-10-01-status-cycle123-relocate.md:28`). Nothing in cycles 128 to 132 touched any of that.

## 7. Termination

Shortest path, unchanged for the fourth review:

1. Write one page describing `D1_s1_disp_20260927_041648.vi` in the user's terms. No LabVIEW needed.
2. Ask for one supervised bead run at 8 and 15 beads, minutes long.
3. Add the R8 frame-rate control to that VI and run 150 Hz once.
4. Resume the ring chain only if step 3 loses frames, and only on the loop the numbers name.

Abandon or park: P3b-2 and P4 to P6 until step 3 has a number, the rebase and route-check plumbing, the memory model, Error List pinning on broken beds, and the interface-contract procedure.

The project's own rule says a repeated outcome verdict means stop and re-plan with the user. This is the fourth repetition. The steering card has demonstrably not changed the next act. The honest move is to stop the runner and show the user the five-review table above.

